-- Q02: QUALITY-2 explanation (966 -> 2423 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$The scenario implies that either the Manage Quality process or the Control Quality process is underway. Both root cause analysis (RCA) and cause-and-effect diagrams can be used as tools and techniques in these processes. However, RCA is an example of the data analysis technique, while the cause-and-effect diagram is an example of the data representation technique. The question reads, "The engineers recommend using a data analysis technique that can help determine the underlying reasons for the performance gaps." The RCA is an analytical technique used to determine the basic underlying reason that causes a variance, defect, or risk, making the RCA the best answer to the question asked. Note, the cause-and-effect diagram is a data representation technique that can be used in an RCA to help identify the main or root cause of the problem. However, on its own, the cause-and-effect diagram does not determine 'what' ultimately caused the problem. Details for Each Option: A Root cause analysis (RCA) Correct. Root cause analysis is a data analysis technique used to determine the basic underlying reason that causes a variance, defect, or risk. When all root causes for a problem are removed, the problem does not recur. Of the choices provided, the RCA best addresses the project manager's need to understand what caused the performance gaps and is, therefore, the best answer to the question asked. B Scatter diagram Incorrect. A scatter diagram is an example of the data representation technique that can be used as part of the Manage Quality process and/or Control Quality process. The application of this technique results in a graph that shows the relationships between two variables, for example, the planned performance vs. the actual performance. The graph, however, does not help determine the underlying reasons for the performance gaps as described in the scenario. C Control chart Incorrect. Control charts are used to determine whether or not a process is stable or has a predictable performance. Control charts are not used to figure out the cause of an issue but rather to identify issues that need to be investigated. D Performance reviews Incorrect. Performance reviews measure, compare, and analyze the quality metrics defined by the Plan Quality Management process against actual results. A performance review can identify an issue that needs to be resolved but does not look into its root cause.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-2$qty23$;

-- Q03: QUALITY-3 explanation (1000 -> 2829 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$The scenario implies that the Manage Quality process is being performed. Scatter diagrams are an example of a data representation technique that can be used during this process. A scatter diagram is a graph that tracks two variables to determine their relationship. The scatter diagram can help decide if there is a relationship, or correlation, between the testing results and the amount of time the testing device has been out of the refrigerator. For example, the project manager can plot the dependent variable, the results, on the y-axis of a graph and the independent variable, the amount of time the testing device has been out of the refrigerator, on the x-axis. The graph will reveal either a positive correlation, a negative correlation, or no correlation between the two variables. If the scatter diagram shows any relationship between the testing results and the time the testing device has been out of the refrigerator, the information can be used to improve the process of testing with the device. Therefore, using a scatter diagram is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Use control charts Incorrect. Control charts are used to determine whether or not a process is stable or has predictable performance. In other words, control charts can be used to track repetitive activities to determine whether or not a process is giving results that are accurate and precise. The project team has already established that the results are precise, but not always accurate, likely by using control charts. However, the project manager wants to determine if there is a correlation between two variables, the amount of time the device has been unrefrigerated before testing, and the results obtained during that testing. Control charts do not demonstrate a relationship between two variables. B Use a scatter diagram Correct. A scatter diagram is a graph that tracks two variables to determine their relationship. For example, by using a scatter diagram, the project manager can decide if there is a relationship, or correlation, between the testing results and the amount of time the device has been out of the refrigerator. C Update the risk repository Incorrect. 'Risk repository' is a made-up term. Even if this was a real project artifact, merely updating the 'risk repository' would not help the project manager confirm or reject her suspicions. D Update the lessons learned register Incorrect. The lessons learned register is updated with information on challenges encountered during the project and how they could have been avoided. At this point, it is not clear what is causing the inaccuracies. Once the project manager has determined the root cause of the inaccuracies, the lessons learned register should be updated with the information so it can be used to improve future performance.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-3$qty23$;

-- Q04: QUALITY-4 explanation (435 -> 1553 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$The question implies that you are involved with the Manage Quality process, and updates to the lessons learned register is a process output. In this case, you have discovered a new tool that will save a lot of time and greatly improve accuracy, and it is important to share the discovery. The lessons learned register serves as the record for everything you and the team discover that can help improve project performance. Details for Each Option: A Lesson learned register Correct. The lessons learned register serves as the record for any lessons learned that can help improve project performance. A new tool that can reduce errors and rework should be detailed there. B Requirements documentation Incorrect. Requirements documentation provides descriptions of how individual requirements meet the business need for the project. Unless specific project tools are required by the customer or sponsor, they would not be detailed here. Based on the scenario, the tool has been discovered by you during project execution, suggesting the tool was not specified in the requirements. C Test and evaluation documents Incorrect. Test and evaluation documents describe the activities used to determine if the product or result meets quality objectives. These documents do not typically capture details on the tools to be used. D Resource management plan Incorrect. The resource management plan describes how resources will be acquired, allocated, monitored and controlled. It does not typically include details on individual tools used during project execution.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-4$qty23$;

-- Q05: QUALITY-5 explanation (1000 -> 2802 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$According to the scenario provided, you are carrying out the Manage Quality process. A histogram is an example of the data representation technique that can be used during this process. A histogram is a vertical bar chart that shows a graphical representation of numerical data. The question is asking you to focus on the most significant causes. You have the data to display the causes and rank them based on the frequency they occur to identify the vital few causes responsible for most of your delays, thus making the histogram the best answer to the question asked. Additionally, the causes can be sorted by their frequency to identify the top cause(s). This sorting turns the simple histogram into what is called a Pareto chart. Although it is not mentioned in the current edition of the PMBOK ® Guide , the Pareto chart is a type of histogram that displays causes, sorted so the most frequent causes are first, which helps you prioritize what will give you the best return on investment in addressing the top causes. For example, potential categories may include causes such as 'weather', 'mechanical issues', 'security', 'other'. The corresponding bars are then plotted on the horizontal axis in descending order of magnitude starting with the source that most frequently contributes to the flight delays and going down to the source, such as 'other', that has the least number of occurrences. Then, the Pareto principle of 80/20 can be applied to focus on the most significant 20% sources that contribute to the 80% of the flight delays. Details for Each Option: A Scatter diagram Incorrect. A scatter diagram is a graph that shows the relationships between two variables, while in the scenario, you have multiple causes that resulted in flight delays. Additionally, the scatter diagram does not focus on the most significant cause, as asked by the question. B Control chart Incorrect. Control charts are used to show whether a process is stable or not. The data you have allows you to determine how many minutes each flight varied from being on time for the last three years, both early and late. However, although a control chart shows variances in such values, it does not focus on the most significant cause. C Histogram Correct. A histogram is a vertical bar chart that can show the number of defects per deliverable, a ranking of the cause of defects, the number of times each process is noncompliant, or other representations of project or product defects. To rank the causes, you can use the data you have on the frequency each occurred. D Cause-and-effect diagram Incorrect. A cause-and-effect diagram, also known as an Ishikawa diagram or fishbone diagram, is used to find the root cause of a problem. However, a cause-and-effect diagram does not focus on the frequency of the causes.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-5$qty23$;

-- Q06: QUALITY-6 explanation (523 -> 1605 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$This question is describing the iteration retrospective meeting. One indication is that the meeting is only being attended by agile project team members. Retrospectives are typically closed to include only team members in order to encourage candid discussion and openness. Some team members may be intimidated by the presence of outsiders and may not contribute as readily. The discussion of something specific that did not go well during the iteration and a possible solution is a classic retrospective topic. Details for Each Option: A Iteration planning meeting Incorrect. The purpose of the iteration planning meeting is for the development team to commit to the completion of a set of those items in the product backlog that are highest-ranked in terms of customer value and risk. B Daily standup meeting Incorrect. When thinking about the daily standup meeting, remember the term 'synchronize'. This meeting is used to synchronize the activities of an agile team to ensure they are all working toward a common iteration goal. C Iteration review meeting Incorrect. The iteration review meeting is a product-oriented meeting held at the end of the iteration to demonstrate product components completed within that iteration. D Iteration retrospective meeting Correct. The iteration retrospective is a process-oriented agile project team meeting that is held at the end of each iteration. The purpose of the iteration retrospective is to reflect on the most significant events that have occurred during the current iteration in order to make decisions on how to improve processes for future iterations.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-6$qty23$;

-- Q07: QUALITY-7 explanation (847 -> 2834 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$In agile projects, the product owner is the individual ultimately responsible for the direction of the product. Product owners prioritize work in the backlog based on value to the customer. Product owners work with the project teams daily by providing product feedback and setting direction on the next piece of functionality to be developed by the team in the upcoming sprint. The sprint review is a meeting where the project team demonstrates the product increment to the product owner and relevant stakeholders. The product owner makes the final call on whether the work demonstrated at the review is acceptable to the business and if it meets acceptance criteria. Therefore, if the product owner misses the sprint review, it will be unclear whether the product increment developed by the team during the sprint is accepted or not. Details for Each Option: A It will be unclear whether the product increment developed by the team during the sprint is accepted or not Correct. The sprint review meeting is the opportunity for the team to demonstrate the product increment they developed during the sprint. One of the responsibilities of the product owner is to define what should be developed in each sprint. Therefore, without the product owner attending the sprint review, the team will be unable to determine if the product increment they developed during the sprint is accepted or not. B The team will be unable to determine if the process they used to develop the product needs improvement Incorrect. The determination as to whether the process the team uses to develop the product needs improvement or not is made at the sprint retrospective meeting, not the sprint review meeting described in the scenario. Besides, it is the team, not the product owner, who determines how to improve the development process. C There will be no significant consequences as a result of the product owner missing one sprint review meeting Incorrect. A typical sprint in an agile project lasts for 2 weeks. Without the product owner attending the sprint review, the team would be unable to determine if the product increment they developed during the sprint is accepted or not, implying the work done over the 2 weeks could be a waste of time and project resources. Therefore, saying that there will be no significant consequences as a result of the product owner missing one sprint review meeting is an understatement. D It will be impossible to determine how much value has been added by the project team during the latest sprint Incorrect. Prior to the sprint, the product owner considers the value of each user story when prioritizing the backlog. What the product owner determines at the end of the sprint (at the sprint review meeting) is the number of story points 'earned' by the development team, but not how much value has been added.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-7$qty23$;

-- Q08: QUALITY-8 explanation (948 -> 2498 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$The project manager's conversation with the department manager suggests the possible cause for the department's slow progress is the duplicate testing policy; however, it is just an assumption. The project manager needs to be proactive and take action. An Ishikawa diagram, which can be used for data analysis, is also known as a cause-and-effect diagram, fishbone diagram, or why-why diagram. This type of diagram breaks down the causes of the problem statement into discrete branches, e.g., activities taking longer than planned, helping to identify the primary or root cause of the problem. Using an Ishikawa diagram will help determine the real root cause for the project to fall behind schedule. This could be the duplicate testing or something else. The root cause analysis will help determine the real underlying reason and enable the project manager to make an informed decision on what actions are required to fix the problem. Details for Each Option: A Do nothing and let the department continue with their practice Incorrect. Doing nothing and letting the department continue their practice of duplicate testing will likely keep the project behind schedule. At this point, the project manager 'suspects' the duplicate testing is the cause of the project delay. To confirm (or reject) this suspicion, an analysis should be made. B Perform data analysis using an Ishikawa diagram Correct. A good project manager is proactive and should look for a root cause and solution for a problem even if it means trying to improve another department's processes. An Ishikawa or cause-and-effect diagram can be used to identify the root cause of the department's slow progress. The schedule delay may or may not be due to the duplicate testing being done by that department. C File a formal complaint to the project management office (PMO) Incorrect. First, it is unclear what role (supportive, controlling, or directive) does the PMO play in the organization. Second, regardless of the role, filing a complaint (either formal or informal) does not sound like a reasonable action or an acceptable project management practice under the circumstances described by the scenario. D Adjust the project schedule to reflect the delays Incorrect. Merely adjusting the schedule would not help the project manager determine the real reason for why the project is behind schedule. The project manager should look for the root cause of the problem and attempt to fix it, rather than react and adjust the schedule.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-8$qty23$;

-- Q09: QUALITY-9 explanation (1000 -> 2998 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$The question implies that the project manager is carrying out the processes of the Project Quality Management Knowledge Area. A lessons learned register is used as an input to and an output from many of those processes. The lessons learned register captures knowledge gained during the project so that it can be used in the current project and entered into the lessons learned repository. According to the scenario described, in addition to using the lessons learned register, the project manager is looking for further options to achieve the project goals of improving the quality of the goods manufactured by the company and reducing waste produced during the manufacturing process. The Deming cycle (also known as the plan-do-check-act cycle) takes an iterative and incremental approach to quality management which can reduce variations in a process (such as the manufacturing process). Additionally, with reduced variations in the goods produced, fewer of the finished goods will fall outside of the specification limits which will reduce waste from the scrapping of rejected goods. Thus, to achieve the project objectives, of the available choices, the implementation of the Deming cycle will have the greatest positive impact on improving quality and reducing waste and is, therefore, the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Conduct a sensitivity analysis during the Control Quality process Incorrect. A sensitivity analysis is an analysis technique to determine which individual project risks or other sources of uncertainty have the most potential impact on project outcomes, by correlating variations in project outcomes with variations in elements of a quantitative risk analysis model. Sensitivity analysis is helpful in monitoring project risks but is not applicable to improving quality. B Create an iteration burndown chart for the project Incorrect. An iteration burndown chart tracks the work that remains to be completed in the iteration backlog. An iteration burndown chart is typically used to control schedule but would not be particularly useful in improving quality. Additionally, an iteration burndown chart is created, as the name implies, for an iteration, not for a project. C Implement the Deming cycle with the project team Correct. The Deming cycle is also known as the plan-do-check-act cycle. The Deming cycle takes an iterative and incremental approach to quality management which can reduce variations in a process, thus improving quality and reducing waste. D Tighten the specification limits of the existing production process Incorrect. Tightening the specification limits will improve the quality of the goods produced by reducing variations for the products that meet the higher quality standards. However, taking this action alone will increase waste as more products will fail to meet the quality standards. The question is asking about continuous improvement and a reduction of waste which should be addressed at the process level.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-9$qty23$;

-- Q10: QUALITY-10 explanation (401 -> 1461 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$Cause-and-effect diagrams are also known as fishbone diagrams, why-why diagrams, or Ishikawa diagrams. This type of diagram breaks down the causes of the problem statement identified into discrete branches, helping to identify the main or root cause of the problem. Of the available choices, only the why-why diagram breaks down the causes of an identified problem into discrete branches. Details for Each Option: A Pareto diagram Incorrect. A Pareto diagram is a type of histogram which shows how many results were generated by each identified cause. While a Pareto diagram displays the causes of the problems, it does not break the causes down into discrete branches. B Matrix diagram Incorrect. A matrix diagram is a quality management and control tool used to perform data analysis within the organizational structure created in the matrix. Matrix diagrams are not used to identify the cause of a problem. C Tornado diagram Incorrect. Tornado diagram is a special type of bar chart used in sensitivity analysis for comparing the relative importance of the variables. Tornado diagrams have nothing to do with the cause-and-effect diagrams described in the scenario. D Why-why diagram Correct. Cause-and-effect diagrams are also known as fishbone diagrams, why-why diagrams, or Ishikawa diagrams. This type of diagram breaks down the causes of the problem statement identified into discrete branches, helping to identify the main or root cause of the problem.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-10$qty23$;

-- Q11: QUALITY-11 explanation (482 -> 2270 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$Design for X (DfX) is a set of technical guidelines that may be applied during the design of a product for the optimization of a specific aspect of the design. In this scenario, technical guidelines are being applied to design a new modular home that optimizes its manufacturability. DfX provides the most accurate and precise definition of the technique being used in this scenario and is, therefore, of the available choices, is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Problem solving Incorrect. Problem solving entails finding solutions for issues or challenges, which is certainly an aspect of what the project manager is doing in this scenario by looking for ways to improve the manufacturability of the product. However, another answer choice more accurately describes the technique used in this situation. B Design for X Correct. Design for X is a technique of the Manage Quality process whereby a person follows certain technical guidelines that may be applied during the design of a product for the optimization of a specific aspect of the design (in this case, manufacturability). In this scenario, the goal is to design a home that is easily and economically manufactured. Using the DfX, the project manager implements certain design decisions (e.g., reducing the total number of parts) in order to accomplish this goal. C Change request Incorrect. A change request is a formal proposal to modify a document, deliverable, or baseline. In this scenario, however, the project manager is not requesting a change to an existing product but rather incorporating new technical guidelines to improve manufacturability into the design of a new product. D Data gathering Incorrect. Data gathering, within the context of quality management as implied by the question, generally refers to a set of tools or techniques (e.g., checklists, process analysis, root cause analysis, alternatives analysis, etc.) used to identify underlying problems, variances, risks, or defects and ensure that project requirements have been met. In this scenario, however, the focus is on improving the manufacturability of the product using a set of technical guidelines to enhance that aspect of product development - an illustration of the Design for X technique.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-11$qty23$;

-- Q12: QUALITY-12 explanation (618 -> 1739 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$Experience has shown that the most significant difference between mature and immature agile teams lies in their commitment to ruthless testing. The objective of ruthless testing is to ensure that product quality remains high throughout the development process. The closer a team comes to running tested features (RTF) every iteration, the more effective it will be. Higher-performance agile teams are those that embrace ruthless testing. While a growing number of software developers use test-first development, ruthless testing is a better generic term that is widely applicable to all types of products. Details for Each Option: A Ruthless testing ensures that quality is added on rather than built into the development process Incorrect. The opposite is true in this case. Ruthless testing ensures that quality is built into the development process rather than added on to it. B Ruthless testing is used to ensure that product quality remains high throughout the development process Correct. The main objective of ruthless testing is to ensure that product quality remains high throughout the development process. C The closer a team comes to running tested features every iteration, the less effective it will be Incorrect. The closer a team comes to running tested features (RTF) every iteration, the more effective it will be. Higher-performance agile teams are those that embrace ruthless testing. D Ruthless testing contributes to creating adaptable products because finding faults later in the project reduces the cost of change Incorrect. Ruthless testing contributes to the goal of creating adaptable products because finding faults early, rather than later, when there is still time to correct them, reduces the cost of change.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-12$qty23$;

-- Q13: QUALITY-13 explanation (470 -> 1496 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $qty23$When planning your project’s quality, you can benchmark your project against those of other projects. This technique relies on the similarity of your current and historical projects and/or project deliverables to have a meaningful comparison. In finding comparable project standards and measurements, your project has a point of comparison, which can aid in setting targets to aim or associate with, if seeking to meet or surpass another project’s standard. Details for Each Option: A Benchmarking Correct. Benchmarking is the tool and technique best used in this situation to capture metrics and standards from comparable projects. Comparing your project’s quality standards against similar projects is a good start for establishing a point of comparison. B Brainstorming Incorrect. Discussing the standards of other projects and throwing out ideas for this project may be useful, but it is not the best method to capture results and plans that already exist from other projects. C Cost-benefit analysis Incorrect. Cost-benefit analysis is not related to planning the quality management of a project and its comparison to other similar projects. D Analogous estimating Incorrect. When estimating project costs, the project team can use the analogous estimating technique to assist in acquiring estimates based on other similar projects within the company. Nonetheless, in this instance, we seek information on how to level or place our project’s quality standards and metrics to measure against.$qty23$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $qty23$QUALITY-13$qty23$;
